    "en": "Step 1, A to B: isothermal expansion at the hot temperature T1. The gas absorbs Q1 from the source. Step 2, B to C: adiabatic expansion. No heat flows; the gas cools from T1 to T2. Step 3, C to D: isothermal compression at T2, rejecting Q2 to the sink. Step 4, D to A: adiabatic compression back to T1. For a Carnot engine Q2 over Q1 equals T2 over T1, so the efficiency depends only on the two temperatures, in kelvin. Between 500 kelvin and 300 kelvin: efficiency 40 percent. From 1000 joule of heat, 400 joule of work at best. Carnot's theorem: no engine between the same two temperatures beats a Carnot engine, and every reversible engine has the same efficiency."
